  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |",
  "worked_solution": "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, $B$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 11 \\\\ -8 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
